Before discussing what Elon Musk’s Starlink said at 10:57 p.m. on October 16, I would first like to point out that people in India are buying #RollsRoyce, #Bugatti, #MercedesBenz, #BMW, #Lamborghini, #Ferrari, #Bentley, and other expensive cars available in the country. This clearly shows that there are people in #India who can afford luxury products and services, including high-quality internet services from Elon Musk’s Starlink.

So, let us now discuss what Elon Musk’s Starlink said through its X (formerly Twitter) account at 10:57 p.m. on October 16:

“We have worked closely with regulators, industry and operators in India for 5+ years, and built a network of dedicated systems, gateways, and other infrastructure all in-country to exclusively serve Indian traffic safely and securely—all with the requisite government approvals.”

Starlink also said it was ready to connect India with high-speed internet.

Forget about Elon Musk’s Starlink for a moment. Believe me or not, #Airtel, led by #SunilBhartiMittal, and #Reliance, led by #MukeshAmbani, appear to be struggling to compete with #ElonMusk and his products. If Airtel or Reliance can compete with Starlink or any of Elon Musk’s products, they should hold a press conference and showcase their achievements to the world.

By the way, #Airtel and #Reliance purchase technology and equipment from foreign companies such as #Nokia, #Ericsson, #ZTE, #AlcatelLucent, #Cisco, and many others to provide telecommunications services to their customers.

Lastly, both companies have received substantial support from the current Narendra Modi-led government, just as they received considerable support under the Congress-led government before 2014. Therefore, the question is whether these companies are doing enough to develop homegrown technologies and demonstrate genuine innovation, regardless of which political party is in power.

By the way, #Airtel owner #SunilBhartiMittal’s father, #SatPaulMittal, was a politician who served as a member of the #RajyaSabha for three terms between 1976 and 1992. He was a member of the Congress party and served as the secretary of the #Punjab Pradesh Congress Committee in 1972.

Meanwhile, #DhirubhaiAmbani, the founder of Reliance and father of its current owner, Mukesh Ambani, maintained a close working relationship with the Congress (I) leadership, madam #IndiraGandhi. Following his father's footsteps, #MukeshAmbani also maintained relationships with top Congress leaders until 2014. Since then, his relationship with the Congress leadership appears to have experienced ups and downs.

Today, both #SunilBhartiMittal and #MukeshAmbani appear to be maintaining relationships with leaders from both the #BJP and the #Congress. From a business perspective, maintaining relationships across political parties may help companies protect their commercial interests and profitability, regardless of which party is in power.

However, I do not have evidence to establish the nature of their private political relationships or to prove that these relationships directly influence their business decisions. My observations are based on what I have seen and heard on the ground. Private meetings are not always accessible to journalists, and we cannot record or independently verify every conversation that takes place behind closed doors. Therefore, I am presenting these views as observations rather than proven allegations.

